{"title":"Work","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Work</b> is a physical quantity defined as the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Force\" title=\"Force\">force</a> exerted on an object over a certain distance. In the case of a constant force <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> over a distance <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the amount of work done is found using the equation:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/7/3d7495672041211bc1579ea4ace32a8c53398bd1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[W=Fd\\cos\\theta\\]\" width=\"105\" height=\"13\" />\nWhere <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/c/10cb764f88509fb1c8012366993fdbee98f31bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$W$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" /> is the work done (J), <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the force (N), <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the distance (m), and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the angle between the direction of force and the direction of movement.\nThis also implies that if a force on an object is perpendicular to the direction in which the object is moving, there is no work done. The most common uses of this fact are in that gravity does not do work on an object on a flat surface and that a rope does not do work on an object experiencing <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Centripetal_motion&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Centripetal motion (page does not exist)\">centripetal motion</a>.\n</p><p>Additionally, the amount of work done on an object is equal to the change in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Kinetic_energy\" title=\"Kinetic energy\">kinetic energy</a>. That is,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/6/e9696d5a1f09cbf43fcc25a115a0f1c3dbbbec5c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[W_{net}=\\Delta KE\\]\" width=\"106\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=SI\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"SI\">SI</a> unit of work is the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Joule\" title=\"Joule\">Joule</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Work&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Force\" title=\"Force\">Force</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Power\" title=\"Power\">Power</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Joule\" title=\"Joule\">Joule</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Work&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018070112\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.012 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.024 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 54/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 253/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function